Generic methods.

A class isn't the only thing that can have a type parameter — a single method can declare its own. A generic method is parameterised independently of its class, which means even a plain, non-generic class, or a static utility, can have one perfectly type-safe generic method.

The syntax is a type parameter in angle brackets, placed just before the return type. That leading angle-T is what makes the method generic, and T is then usable in the parameters and the return. Take first-or-null: static, angle-T, returning a T, taking a list of T; it returns null if the list is empty, or the first element otherwise. Or repeat: static, angle-T, returning a list of T, taking an item of type T and a count; it builds a list with that item added n times. First-or-null works for a list of string, or a list of user, returning the matching type — no casts, no overloads. And notice these are static, and the class holding them needn't be generic at all; the method owns T.

The lovely part is type inference — you rarely write the type yourself. The compiler infers the method's type argument from the arguments you pass, so calls look completely ordinary. First-or-null of a list of "a" and "b" infers T as string. Repeat of three, five times, infers T as integer. You almost never state T explicitly. And when inference genuinely can't tell — which is rare, like an empty context — you can supply an explicit type witness before the method name. For example, collections dot, angle-string, empty-list, forces T to be string. You'll see that occasionally in library code; day to day, just let inference do it.

So when do you make the class generic, versus a single method? Make the class generic when the parameter describes the whole object's state, held across many methods — a box of T, a list of E, a cache of K and V. The type is fixed once, at construction. Make a method generic when the type is local to just one call, and doesn't need to live on the object — a utility that transforms or inspects whatever type it's handed. Collections dot sort, list dot of, optional dot map, and most of the collections utility class, are all generic methods. A good tell: if two calls to the same operation would naturally use different types, it wants to be a generic method, not a generic class.

And here's the real power. Because a method can name several parameters and reuse them, it can express relationships between types that a signature otherwise couldn't. Consider an invert method: static, angle-K-V, returning a map from V to K, taking a map from K to V. It swaps every key and value. The signature guarantees that the output's key type is the input's value type, and vice versa — the compiler checks it, and callers read the contract straight from the types. That ability to tie inputs to outputs is what makes generic methods the backbone of the utility APIs you'll write. So far, T has been unconstrained; next, we give it bounds, so a method can require its type to actually do something.
